  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121632,
      "SummaryText": "The San Antonio Water System (SAWS) is a public entity owned by the City of San Antonio (CoSA) in San Antonio, Texas.  SAWS provides drinking water and wastewater services to approximately one million people in urbanized sections of Bexar County.  The Water Delivery system conveys ground water to customers through elevated and ground storage tanks, pumps stations and transmission and distribution mains. The system consists of approximately 5,000 miles of transmission and distribution mains, an aquifer storage and recovery (ASR) facility, and a brackish water desalination facility.  The wastewater system conveys wastewater from customers through pipes, lift stations, pumps stations and transmission, distribution mains and into wastewater treatment facilities.  Gaseous chlorine is used to provide disinfection and anhydrous sulfur dioxide is added to the disinfected wastewater to remove excess chlorine residual prior to discharging effluent into the receiving stream.  \nChlorine and Sulfur Dioxide are categorized by the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) as extremely hazardous substances (EHS); thus, SAWS has developed a Risk Management Plan (RMP) for management and prevention of risks associated with the storage, handling, and processing of chlorine and sulfur dioxide in accordance with 40 CFR Part 68, Section 112 (r) of the Clean Air Act.  This RMP is specific for Steven Clouse Water Recycling Center."
    }
  ]
